  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Suppose $(a,b,c)$ is a primitive Pythagorean triple.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that precisely one of $a$ and $b$ is divisible by $2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Exactly one leg is even.\n\nIn Euclid’s formula, $m$ and $n$ have opposite parity, so $m^2-n^2$ is odd and $2mn$ is even.",
          "worked": "By Euclid’s formula, the legs are $m^2-n^2$ and $2mn$, where $m$ and $n$ have opposite parity. Their squares also have opposite parity, so $m^2-n^2$ is odd. The other leg, $2mn$, is even. Therefore precisely one of $a$ and $b$ is divisible by $2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that precisely one of $a$ and $b$ is divisible by $3$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Exactly one leg is divisible by $3$.\n\nConsider whether either parameter in Euclid’s formula is divisible by $3$; nonzero squares have remainder $1$.",
          "worked": "Use $a=m^2-n^2$ and $b=2mn$, with $\\operatorname{GCD}(m,n)=1$. Squares have remainder $0$ or $1$ on division by $3$.\n\nIf one of $m,n$ is divisible by $3$, the other is not. Then $b=2mn$ is divisible by $3$, while $a=m^2-n^2$ has remainder $1$ or $2$.\n\nIf neither is divisible by $3$, both squares have remainder $1$. Then $a=m^2-n^2$ is divisible by $3$, while $b=2mn$ is not.\n\nThus precisely one of the two legs is divisible by $3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that precisely one of $a$ and $b$ is divisible by $4$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Exactly one leg is divisible by $4$.\n\nOne of $m,n$ is even, so $4\\mid2mn$; the other leg is odd.",
          "worked": "The parameters $m,n$ have opposite parity, so one is even. Hence $mn$ is even and $2mn$ is divisible by $4$. The other leg, $m^2-n^2$, is odd and therefore cannot be divisible by $4$. Thus precisely one of $a$ and $b$ is divisible by $4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q10-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Show that precisely one of $a$, $b$ and $c$ is divisible by $5$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Exactly one of $a,b,c$ is divisible by $5$.\n\nUse Euclid’s formula and the possible square remainders $0,1,4$ modulo $5$.",
          "worked": "Use $a=m^2-n^2$, $b=2mn$ and $c=m^2+n^2$, where $\\operatorname{GCD}(m,n)=1$. Squares have remainder $0$, $1$ or $4$ on division by $5$.\n\nIf one of $m,n$ is divisible by $5$, the other is not. Then $b$ is divisible by $5$, while $a$ and $c$ have nonzero remainders.\n\nIf neither parameter is divisible by $5$, their square remainders are $1$ or $4$. If these remainders are equal, $a$ is divisible by $5$, while $c$ has remainder $2$ or $3$. If the remainders differ, $c$ is divisible by $5$, while $a$ has remainder $2$ or $3$. In both cases $b$ is not divisible by $5$.\n\nThese cases cover all possibilities, so precisely one of $a,b,c$ is divisible by $5$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      }
    ]
  },
